# Accessibility

## Semantic HTML
Use the right element for the job. Never fake interactivity with divs.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — div pretending to be a button
<div onClick={handleClick} className="btn">Generate</div>

// ✅ GOOD — real button
<button onClick={handleClick} className="btn">Generate</button>
```

- `<button>` for actions
- `<a>` for navigation (with `href`)
- `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` for form inputs
- `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`, `<header>` for page structure
- `<h1>` through `<h6>` in order — never skip heading levels

## Every form input has a label

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — no label, screen readers can't identify the field
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter brief..." />

// ✅ GOOD — associated label
<label htmlFor="brief">Brief</label>
<input id="brief" type="text" placeholder="Enter brief..." />

// ✅ ALSO GOOD — visually hidden label when design doesn't show one
<label htmlFor="brief" className="sr-only">Brief</label>
<input id="brief" type="text" placeholder="Enter brief..." />
```

## Keyboard navigation
- Every interactive element must be reachable with Tab
- Pressing Enter or Space on a focused button must trigger its action
- Never remove the focus outline without providing an alternative visible focus indicator

## Images
- Meaningful images: `<img alt="Description of what the image shows" />`
- Decorative images: `<img alt="" role="presentation" />`
- Never leave `alt` undefined

## Color
Never use color as the only way to convey information (e.g. red text for errors). Always pair color with text, icons, or other visual cues.
